The trick is to keep insertion order while counting: group the characters with Collectors.groupingBy, supplying a LinkedHashMap so entries stay in first-seen order, and count each group with Collectors.counting(). Then stream the map entries, keep the first one whose count is one, and return its key, or null when every character repeats. The counting pass touches each character once and the scan touches at most the distinct characters, so the whole thing is linear in the length of the string, with memory proportional to the number of distinct characters. A plain HashMap would give the right counts but the wrong 'first', because iteration order is not insertion order — that is the most common mistake. A simpler two-pass alternative counts with a map and then scans the original string for the first character whose count is one; it is equally linear and easier to read, and it handles the empty-string and all-repeated cases by returning null.
Example solution:

```java
import java.util.LinkedHashMap;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.function.Function;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class FirstNonRepeated {
    public static Character firstNonRepeated(String input) {
        if (input == null || input.isEmpty()) {
            return null;
        }
        Map<Character, Long> counts = input.chars()
                .mapToObj(c -> (char) c)
                .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(
                        Function.identity(), LinkedHashMap::new, Collectors.counting()));
        return counts.entrySet().stream()
                .filter(e -> e.getValue() == 1)
                .map(Map.Entry::getKey)
                .findFirst()
                .orElse(null);
    }
}
```

Use a dummy node at the front so you never need special code for the first element: keep a tail pointer starting at the dummy, and while both lists have nodes, link the smaller current node after the tail and advance that list and the tail. When one list runs out, link the rest of the other list as it is — it is already sorted, so no further comparison is needed — and return dummy.next as the merged head. The dummy node is the key idea: without it, the first link needs a separate branch and the code gets messy. The merge reuses the existing nodes, so it uses constant extra space beyond the dummy node, and it touches each node once, giving linear time in the combined length. Using <= when comparing keeps the merge stable, so equal values keep their original relative order. A recursive version is shorter but risks a stack overflow on long lists, so prefer the iterative version in production code; also handle null inputs, since a null list just means the other list is the answer.
Example solution:

```java
public class MergeSortedLists {
    static class Node {
        int value;
        Node next;

        Node(int value) {
            this.value = value;
        }
    }

    public static Node merge(Node first, Node second) {
        Node dummy = new Node(0);
        Node tail = dummy;
        while (first != null && second != null) {
            if (first.value <= second.value) {
                tail.next = first;
                first = first.next;
            } else {
                tail.next = second;
                second = second.next;
            }
            tail = tail.next;
        }
        tail.next = (first != null) ? first : second;
        return dummy.next;
    }
}
```
